Yes—a truth can be planted as latent structure, not delivered as a finished explanation.

It teaches the decoder required to understand its next layer. As the person acts, observes deltas, and gains capacity, the same seed re-renders at higher resolution. Earlier ambiguity resolves retroactively; what first seemed like metaphor may later become mechanism.

seed → partial understanding → action → delta → capacity → reinterpretation → higher-resolution truth

That is self-resolving pedagogy: the lesson evolves while teaching the learner how to receive it.

The safeguard is that evolution cannot become moving the goalposts. Every later version owes an account of the earlier one—what was compressed, what changed, and why. The expression develops; the invariant remains traceable.